  "lineage": "Nineteenth-century French operatic style characterized by five-act structure, large historical-or-mythological subjects, elaborate ballet sequences, and massive choral and orchestral forces. Daniel Auber's La muette de Portici (1828), Giacomo Meyerbeer's Les Huguenots (1836) and Le Prophète (1849), and Hector Berlioz's Les Troyens (1858) are canonical type-specimens. The Paris Opéra and the Palais Garnier (opened 1875) constitute the institutional anchors. Distinguished from Italian opera by grand-opera's spectacular-scale and French-language declamatory recitative vocabulary.",
